{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"plan","namespace":"","artifact_type":"","name":"Pdea64bb9"},"owner_reference":"#3182","content":"{\"plan_id\":\"Pdea64bb9\",\"feature\":\"backlog-add-silent-failure\",\"version\":\"1.0.0\",\"description\":\"\",\"goal\":\"Make a failed GitHub issue creation in backlog_add reach the caller: _try_create_github_issue and _try_create_backend_issue_ref return the reason they already compute, add_item copies it into result[\\\"error\\\"], the CLI exits non-zero on it, and tests plus the create workflow doc codify the new three-outcome contract (created / local-only-and-fine / attempted-and-failed).\",\"context\":\"**Implementation base SHA**: bf4dcd8763805bc9083e71b2bd00bea4d39afd00\\n\\n## Source of truth\\n\\n- Architecture spec: `plan/architect-backlog-add-silent-failure.md` (read via `artifact_read(item_id=3182, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")`). It contains exact signatures, full docstring text, the complete test contract, and exact documentation replacement text. **Follow it precisely; do not re-derive the design.**\\n- Feature context: `plan/feature-context-backlog-add-silent-failure.md`.\\n- Corrected Acceptance Criteria: backlog item #3182, Acceptance Criteria section, entry `2026-09-04T01:32:16.754842Z`. The earlier entry is struck. **The Impact Radius and RT-ICA sections still reference the struck pre-correction wording \\u2014 ignore them on this point.**\\n\\n## The one design decision that governs every task\\n\\n`_try_create_github_issue` has two failure branches that are collapsed today and must be split:\\n\\n| Branch | Condition | Contract |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| GitHub unavailable | `try_get_github(repo) is None` | **Tolerated, silent.** Local-only item is created, `item_ref == \\\"\\\"`, **no** `error` key. This is shipped behavior from #2999 and is exercised by currently-passing tests. |\\n| Creation raised | client obtained, `create_issue_for_item` raises `GithubException`/`BacklogError` | **Reported.** Local-only item is still created, `item_ref == \\\"\\\"`, **and** `result[\\\"error\\\"]` carries `\\\"Issue creation failed: {e}\\\"`. This is the #3182 defect. |\\n\\nSurfacing an error on the *unavailable* branch would break `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_frontend_parity_ops.py`'s `dh_env` fixture (line 99), which explicitly sets `GITHUB_TOKEN=\\\"\\\"` and `GH_TOKEN=\\\"\\\"` and asserts CLI exit code 0. That entire parity suite is the regression guard for this decision.\\n\\n## Call graph (integer-ID backends only; beads unchanged)\\n\\nAlways read `operations.py` lines 1444\\u20131499 end-to-end before implementing:\\n\\n```\\nbacklog_add (MCP, server.py:1377\\u20131406)\\n  \\u2514\\u2500 add_item (operations.py:1528\\u20131637)\\n      \\u2514\\u2500 _try_create_backend_issue_ref (operations.py:1466\\u20131499)\\n         \\u2514\\u2500 _try_create_github_issue (operations.py:1444\\u20131463, integer-ID only)\\n```\\n\\n## Test invocation commands (Phase 6 verification)\\n\\n```bash\\n# Unit-level verification (mocking GitHub, no live credentials needed)\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py::TestAddItem -xvs\\n\\n# Regression guard: parity suite, must stay green\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests/test_frontend_parity_ops.py -xvs -k \\\"add_item or add_parity\\\"\\n\\n# Integration-level (live credentials required; currently red)\\nuv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py::test_l1_add_with_real_issue -xvs\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Context Manifest: Phase 6 (Discovery \\u2192 Planning)\\n\\n### Architecture readiness\\n\\nThe architect spec (artifact type `architect`) is **complete and executable**. It specifies:\\n\\n- **Exact signatures** for three functions: `_try_create_github_issue(\\u2026) -> tuple[int | None, str | None]`, `_try_create_backend_issue_ref(\\u2026) -> tuple[str, str | None]`, and `add_item(\\u2026)` with its result dict construction.\\n- **Two new test cases** with exact mocking setup and assertions, plus one rewritten existing test that codifies the design decision above.\\n- **Two documentation changes**: `backlog_add` MCP tool's docstring, CLI `backlog add` command's post-call check, and `create/start.md`'s workflow Step 5 and Completion criteria.\\n- **No Pydantic schema changes**, no new exception classes, no new fields. The fix reuses `BacklogAddResponse.error: str | None = None` (already present in the model) and populates the already-wired dict key in `add_item`.\\n\\n**Quality vigilance note:** The architect spec includes three Concerns sections. All three are **load-bearing for implementation**:\\n\\n1. **Concern 1**: The groomed Acceptance Criteria are broader than this spec implements. They say *\\\"populate error when creation fails **or** GitHub is unavailable\\\"*, but the spec (correctly) narrows to only the \\\"creation fails\\\" case to preserve the parity test suite. **Recommend the Acceptance Criteria entry be corrected during S6 implementation review** to read \\\"GitHub issue creation is attempted and fails\\\" rather than \\\"fails or is unavailable\\\". This is the governing decision that makes all downstream contract shapes correct.\\n\\n2. **Concern 2**: Pre-existing, out-of-scope bug on the beads path \\u2014 `ContentUnavailableError` is not caught by `backlog_add`/CLI `add` exception handlers. This item fixes integer-ID only; recommend a follow-up backlog item for beads.\\n\\n3. **Concern 3**: `test_live_validation.py:196,318` need their assertion messages updated (not spec'd in the file list but implied by the test contract). Flagging so it is not dropped.\\n\\n### Decision boundaries and tensions\\n\\n**OQ-1 (resolved)** \\u2014 The feature context raises: is \\\"GitHub unavailable \\u2192 create local-only item\\\" a failure or degraded success? The architect spec resolves this as **degraded success** (it is tolerated, silent, not an error). This resolves the entire contract shape. The alternative (treating it as a failure) would break the parity test suite and the #2999 feature.\\n\\n**OQ-2 (for Phase 6 execution)** \\u2014 Does the CLI change its exit code? Yes: `typer.Exit(code=1)` when `result.get(\\\"error\\\")` is truthy (only on the \\\"creation failed\\\" branch, never on \\\"unavailable\\\"). This is a small behavioral change documented in the architect spec's CLI section.\\n\\n**OQ-3 (documented, not widened)** \\u2014 Two sibling `return \\\"\\\"` sites exist at operations.py:1494 (beads) and 1497 (unknown string-ID). The architect spec fixes only 1499 (GitHub integer-ID). The beads site is left as-is because `create_beads_issue_for_item` already raises from inside the backend. The 1497 site is also untouched \\u2014 if an unknown string-ID backend is encountered, it's treated the same as beads (silent fallback). This is a documented narrow scope, not an oversight.\\n\\n### What must NOT change (regression guards)\\n\\n- **Beads backend behavior** (`TestAddItemBeadsBackend`, tests 560\\u2013645): untouched. Beads raises `ContentUnavailableError` from inside the backend, not from `_try_create_backend_issue_ref`.\\n- **#2999 guarantee** (`test_add_item_local_only_pending_state_visible_via_list_and_view`, test 435\\u2013459): Local-only item creation on GitHub-unavailable must still succeed and be visible via `list_items`/`view_item`. No error key, exit code 0.\\n- **Parity test suite** (`test_frontend_parity_ops.py`, entire file): All tests that mock `GITHUB_TOKEN=\\\"\\\"` must still pass with exit code 0. This is the regression guard for Concern 1.\\n\\n### Verification strategy\\n\\n**Unit-level (sufficient for this phase)**: Mock `try_get_github` to reach both branches (`return None` for unavailable, `return <Mock>` for success) and `create_issue_for_item` to raise `GithubException`. Assert `result[\\\"error\\\"]` is populated only on the raised case, and absent on the unavailable case. This is already set up in test fixtures.\\n\\n**Live-level (currently blocked)**: The E2E suite (`test_live_validation.py`) has been red for 4+ weeks and is waiting for a real GitHub token and a passing `backlog_add` to diagnose why. Once the fix lands, running this suite will either (a) pass, revealing the entire operation works, or (b) still fail with the actual error visible in `result[\\\"error\\\"]` instead of hidden in `warnings`. Either outcome is better than today's opaque `ValueError: invalid literal for int()`.\\n\\n**Risk note**: Verification depends on CI environment stability (R-5). If the E2E suite still fails after this fix lands, it is not this fix's failure \\u2014 it proves the fix is working by making the error visible.\\n\\n### Implementation sequencing (for S6)\\n\\n1. **Verify** the architect spec's Concern 1 (Acceptance Criteria narrowing) is reviewed and the item wording corrected before code changes.\\n2. **Update signatures** first: `_try_create_github_issue` return type, `_try_create_backend_issue_ref` return type and the call site in `add_item`.\\n3. **Update add_item result dict** construction: capture `issue_error` and conditionally add `result[\\\"error\\\"]`.\\n4. **Update CLI** `backlog add` command: add the post-call check for `result.get(\\\"error\\\")` and `typer.Exit(code=1)`.\\n5. **Rewrite the codifying test** (`test_add_item_item_ref_empty_when_no_github_issue`) to assert the tolerated-fallback case stays error-free.\\n6. **Add the new test** (`test_add_item_error_set_when_issue_creation_raises`) to cover the creation-failed case.\\n7. **Update docstrings** in `backlog_add` MCP tool and `operations` functions per the architect spec.\\n8. **Update workflow documentation** in `create/start.md` Step 5 and Completion criteria per the architect spec.\\n9. **Update test assertions in test_live_validation.py** (lines 196, 318) to include `result.get(\\\"error\\\")` in the message (Concern 3).\\n\\n### Known unknowns / follow-up items\\n\\n- **Why GitHub issue creation actually fails in CI** (OQ-6): Deliberately deferred. Once this fix lands, the answer will be in `result[\\\"error\\\"]`. File as a follow-up backed by diagnostics.\\n- **Broader `out.warn()` usage audit** (OQ-5): Tempting refactor, out of scope. Note for future patterns work.\\n- **BeadsBackend.put_work_item ContentUnavailableError handling** (Concern 2): Pre-existing, blocks nothing here, recommend follow-up.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"\",\"issue\":\"3182\",\"tasks\":[{\"id\":\"T0\",\"title\":\"Capture T0 baseline for the seven structured acceptance criteria\",\"status\":\"complete\",\"agent\":\"dh:t0-baseline-capture\",\"dependencies\":[],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"low\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-typing\",\"python-engineering:python3-testing\",\"fastmcp-creator:fastmcp-creator\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-04T01:53:33.591932+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":\"2026-09-04T03:32:29.471162+00:00\",\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"A registered t0-baseline artifact recording the actual pre-implementation state of each of this plan's seven `acceptance-criteria-structured` check commands, so T9 can prove which criteria changed.\",\"requirements\":\"1. Read `acceptance-criteria-structured` from this plan via `sam_plan(action=\\\"read\\\")`.\\n2. Run each `check-command` verbatim from the repository root and record its exit code and captured output.\\n3. Assemble the results as YAML in memory and register them via `artifact_register` with `content=`, `item_id=3182`, and the t0-baseline artifact type.\\n4. Note in the artifact which criteria were expected to fail at baseline (AC1, AC5, AC6, AC7) and which were expected to pass (AC2, AC3, AC4), and whether each observation matched.\",\"constraints\":\"- Do NOT modify any source, test, or documentation file. This task only observes.\\n- Do NOT treat a non-zero exit as a task failure. Record it.\\n- Do NOT skip a command because its outcome seems predictable. An AC2/AC3/AC4 failure at baseline would mean the tree is already broken and the plan must stop before T1 \\u2014 report that loudly if it happens.\\n- Do NOT create a commit.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- A registered t0-baseline artifact on item 3182. No files modified in the working tree.\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. All seven criteria have a recorded exit code and output excerpt.\\n2. The artifact is registered against item 3182 and readable via `artifact_read`.\\n3. Any divergence from the expected baseline pattern (AC1/AC5/AC6/AC7 fail, AC2/AC3/AC4 pass) is called out explicitly.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. Run each of the seven `check-command` values from the repository root, capturing exit code and output.\\n2. Register the assembled YAML via `artifact_register(item_id=3182, ...)` with `content=`.\\n3. `artifact_read(item_id=3182, artifact_type=\\\"t0-baseline\\\")` \\u2014 confirm the content round-trips.\\n4. `git status --short` \\u2014 confirm the working tree is unchanged.\",\"context_notes\":\"Item id for artifact registration: 3182.\\n\\nNon-zero exit codes are expected here and are not failures. Four criteria are expected to fail at T0 by design \\u2014 AC1 (the test does not exist yet), AC5, AC6, AC7 \\u2014 and three are expected to pass (AC2, AC3, AC4), which is the point: they are regression guards on already-shipped behavior and must still pass at TN.\\n\\nRun every command from the repository root. AC1's `-k` selector matches nothing today, so pytest exits 5 (no tests collected) rather than 1 \\u2014 record the exit code verbatim; do not normalize it.\",\"handoff\":\"Report the seven criteria with actual exit codes, whether each matched its `expected-baseline`, the registered artifact identifier, and an explicit STOP recommendation if AC2, AC3, or AC4 failed at baseline.\",\"analysis_method\":\"none\",\"divergence_notes\":0,\"accuracy_risk\":\"low\",\"is_bookend\":true,\"bookend_type\":\"t0-baseline\"},{\"id\":\"T1\",\"title\":\"Widen _try_create_github_issue / _try_create_backend_issue_ref to return the failure reason and copy it into add_item's result\",\"status\":\"not-started\",\"agent\":\"python-engineering:python-cli-architect\",\"dependencies\":[\"T0\"],\"blocked_by\":[],\"parallelize_with\":[\"T4\",\"T5\"],\"priority\":1,\"complexity\":\"medium\",\"skills\":[\"python-engineering:python3-typing\",\"python-engineering:python3-testing\",\"fastmcp-creator:fastmcp-creator\"],\"created\":\"2026-09-04T01:53:33.591991+00:00\",\"started\":null,\"completed\":null,\"last_activity\":null,\"body\":\"## CoVe checks\\n\\nKey claims to verify before committing:\\n\\n1. **Claim:** `_try_create_github_issue` and `_try_create_backend_issue_ref` have exactly one caller each, so widening their return types is a closed change.\\n   **Question:** Does `grep -rn '_try_create_github_issue\\\\|_try_create_backend_issue_ref' --include='*.py' plugins/development-harness` show any call site outside `operations.py:1498` and `operations.py:1594`?\\n   **Expected evidence:** only those two calls, plus definitions and docstring mentions, plus one docstring reference in `tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py:566`.\\n\\n2. **Claim:** `server.py` needs no code change because `_respond` already routes a result-dict `error` key into `BacklogAddResponse.error`.\\n   **Question:** Read `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:147-190` \\u2014 does `_respond` call `model_validate(payload)` on the merged dict, and is `error` an inherited field on `BacklogAddResponse` (`tool_responses.py:113-117, 212-228`)?\\n   **Revision rule:** if `_respond` filters keys against an allowlist rather than validating the whole dict, this task's scope is wrong \\u2014 stop and report rather than adding a workaround.\\n\\n3. **Claim:** omitting the key (rather than setting `None`) is the house convention.\\n   **Question:** Do other `FallibleToolResponse` subclasses' producers in `operations.py` set `error` only on failure?\\n   **Evidence to collect:** at least one sibling operation's result-dict construction.\\n\\n4. **Claim:** the parity suite exercises the unavailable branch and asserts exit code 0.\\n   **Question:** Does `tests/test_frontend_parity_ops.py:99` set `GITHUB_TOKEN` to `\\\"\\\"` and do `_run_cli` (line 60-61) / `_invoke_cli` (line 77-79) raise `RuntimeError` on non-zero exit?\\n   **Revision rule:** if either is false, the silent-unavailable-branch constraint may be over-cautious \\u2014 report the discrepancy rather than changing the contract unilaterally.\\n\\nReport corrected assumptions and remaining uncertainty in the handoff.\",\"description\":\"\",\"objective\":\"`backlog_core.operations.add_item` returns a dict containing an `\\\"error\\\"` key carrying `\\\"Issue creation failed: {e}\\\"` when an integer-ID backend obtained a GitHub client but `create_issue_for_item` raised \\u2014 and omits that key entirely on success and on the GitHub-unavailable local-only fallback.\",\"requirements\":\"1. Change `_try_create_github_issue`'s return annotation to `tuple[int | None, str | None]` and its three returns to `(number, None)`, `(None, None)` for the unavailable branch, and `(None, reason)` for the raised branch \\u2014 where `reason = f\\\"Issue creation failed: {e}\\\"` is the same string passed to `out.warn`. Replace its docstring with the architect spec's version, which documents all three tuple shapes.\\n2. Change `_try_create_backend_issue_ref`'s return annotation to `tuple[str, str | None]`. Add `, None` to the two string-ID branch returns. Change the final line to capture and forward the error: `issue_num, error = _try_create_github_issue(item_data, repo, out)` then `return (f\\\"#{issue_num}\\\", None) if issue_num else (\\\"\\\", error)`. Replace its docstring with the architect spec's version.\\n3. In `add_item`, unpack at line 1594: `issue_ref, issue_error = _try_create_backend_issue_ref(item_data, repo, out)`.\\n4. After the `result` dict literal, add `if issue_error: result[\\\"error\\\"] = issue_error` before the existing `return {**result, **out.to_dict()}`.\\n5. Append the architect spec's `error` paragraph to `add_item`'s existing `Returns:` docstring block.\\n6. Update `backlog_add`'s docstring in `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (~1377-1406): the existing `Returns:` line \\\"On error, ``error`` is set.\\\" gains the clause given verbatim in the architect spec's \\\"MCP tool `backlog_add`\\\" subsection.\",\"constraints\":\"- Do NOT change `_try_create_github_issue`'s control flow, warning text, or exception tuple. Only the return shape and docstring change.\\n- Do NOT populate `error` on the `try_get_github(...) is None` branch. That branch stays silent \\u2014 see the plan context table.\\n- Do NOT widen `add_item`'s return annotation. `str` is already a member of `dict[str, str | int | bool | list[str]]`.\\n- Do NOT set `result[\\\"error\\\"] = None` on success. Omit the key entirely \\u2014 `BacklogAddResponse` is serialized with `model_dump(exclude_none=True)` and every other `FallibleToolResponse` subclass in the file follows the omit-on-success convention.\\n- Do NOT add Pydantic fields, exception classes, or edit `tool_responses.py`. `BacklogAddResponse.error` already exists.\\n- Do NOT add `error` handling to `server.py`'s code. `_respond` (server.py:147-190) already validates the result dict into the model; only the docstring changes there.\\n- Do NOT touch any test file. T3 owns `test_backlog_core_operations.py`; T4 owns `test_live_validation.py`.\\n- Do NOT rename `Output.record_error` or call a nonexistent `out.error(...)`. `models.py:1413-1424` documents that a `FallibleToolResponse.error` field collides with an inherited same-named method under Pydantic v2; `record_error` is named that way on purpose and is not used by this change.\",\"expected_outputs\":\"- Modified: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`\\n- Modified: `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (docstring only)\",\"acceptance_criteria\":\"1. `_try_create_github_issue` is annotated `-> tuple[int | None, str | None]` and returns a 2-tuple on every path.\\n2. `_try_create_backend_issue_ref` is annotated `-> tuple[str, str | None]` and returns a 2-tuple on all four return sites.\\n3. `add_item` unpacks two values and conditionally sets `result[\\\"error\\\"]`.\\n4. A call where `try_get_github` returns a mock and `create_issue_for_item` raises `GithubException` produces a result dict where `result[\\\"item_ref\\\"] == \\\"\\\"` and `result[\\\"error\\\"].startswith(\\\"Issue creation failed:\\\")`.\\n5. A call where `try_get_github` returns `None` produces a result dict where `result[\\\"item_ref\\\"] == \\\"\\\"` and `\\\"error\\\" not in result`.\\n6. A call where `create_issue_for_item` returns an int produces `result[\\\"item_ref\\\"] == \\\"#<int>\\\"` and `\\\"error\\\" not in result`.\\n7. `uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py` reports no new diagnostics on the changed functions.\\n8. No test file is modified by this task.\",\"verification_steps\":\"1. `uv run ruff check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`\\n2. `uv run ruff format --check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py`\\n3. `uv run ty check plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py`\\n4. `uv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py --no-cov -q` \\u2014 expect a fully green run; existing tests do not yet assert on `error`, so none should change state. Any failure means this task broke something.\\n5. `uv run pytest plugins/development-harness/tests/test_frontend_parity_ops.py --no-cov -q` and `... -q -m integration` \\u2014 both must be green, proving the unavailable branch still exits CLI code 0.\\n6. `git status --short` \\u2014 confirm only `operations.py` and `server.py` are modified.\\n7. `git add plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` then `git commit -m \\\"fix(backlog-core): surface GitHub issue-creation failure reason to add_item callers\\\"`\",\"context_notes\":\"Current source, read and confirmed in this worktree:\\n\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1444-1463` \\u2014 `_try_create_github_issue(...) -> int | None`. Both failure branches `out.warn(...)` and `return None`; the reason string is computed and then discarded.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1466-1499` \\u2014 `_try_create_backend_issue_ref(...) -> str`. Three `return \\\"\\\"` sites: beads falsy nanoid (1494), unknown string-ID backend (1497), and the integer-ID path (1499).\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1594` \\u2014 `issue_ref = _try_create_backend_issue_ref(item_data, repo, out)`.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1627-1637` \\u2014 the `result` dict literal and `return {**result, **out.to_dict()}`.\\n\\nThe architect spec (`artifact_read(item_id=3182, artifact_type=\\\"architect\\\")`, section \\\"Component contracts\\\") gives the complete post-change body and docstring for all three, verbatim. Use that text; it is the contract, not a sketch.\\n\\nWhy the two string-ID `return \\\"\\\"` sites at 1494 and 1497 get `, None` and nothing more: the beads path's real failure signal is `BeadsBackend.put_work_item` raising `ContentUnavailableError` further down `add_item`, which this task does not touch. Feature-context OQ-3 asked whether they should be fixed here; the answer for this item is no \\u2014 see the item's Acceptance Criteria final bullet, which scopes the `ContentUnavailableError` gap to its own backlog item.\",\"handoff\":\"Report: the final bodies of the two changed helpers, the two changed lines in `add_item`, the ruff/ty/pytest command outputs, the CoVe eviden